{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791982442441,"sessionID":"ses_ec58729d2ffeZXCFNW02JwR47g","part":{"partID":"prt_13a79036f001Q0QMiUMiaVTmYH","sessionID":"ses_ec58729d2ffeZXCFNW02JwR47g","messageID":"msg_13a78fe1c0018m9A51BaJa0CJM","type":"tool","id":"toolu_vrtx_011ZEUx1ga83wkP1XFSGgszk","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/private/var/folders/qm/d729jn6n47z5nxpkpf361l9w0000gn/T/agent-eval/v2-regression/cases/case-018-inconsistent-deadlines/artifacts/result.md","content":"# Requirements Elicitation Package\n\n## Understanding\n\n**Requested outcome:** The report service must return a report within a defined\ntime limit whenever a valid report request arrives.\n\n**Proposed system boundary:** The report service — the component that receives\na valid report request at its public interface and returns the corresponding\nreport to the caller.\n\n**Input artifact identification:**\n\n| Label | Text | Kind |\n|-------|------|------|\n| R1 | \"When a valid report request arrives, the report service shall return the report within 1 second.\" | Behavioral obligation (event-driven, with timing constraint) |\n| R2 | \"When a valid report request arrives, the report service shall return the report within 5 seconds.\" | Behavioral obligation (event-driven, with timing constraint) |\n\nBoth R1 and R2 assert the same trigger (`a valid report request arrives`),\nthe same system (`the report service`), and the same response (`return the\nreport`), but they specify contradictory deadlines: **1 second** versus\n**5 seconds**. The user has confirmed that both apply to the same service\nboundary and workload. This is a direct conflict. Neither requirement can be\naccepted as-is; both are held at `needs clarification` until the conflict is\nresolved.\n\n---\n\n## Clarifying Questions\n\n**Q1 (Conflict resolution — blocking):** R1 and R2 require the same response\nfor the same event on the same system but specify different deadlines (1 s and\n5 s). They cannot both be correct as written. Which of the following best\ndescribes the intent?\n\n- **(a) One deadline replaces the other.** If so, which deadline is correct —\n  1 second or 5 seconds — and which requirement should be superseded?\n- **(b) The two deadlines apply to different workload or report classes** (for\n  example, simple vs. complex reports, small vs. large datasets, cached vs.\n  uncached results). If so, how should each class be identified at the service\n  boundary so that a verifier can distinguish them?\n- **(c) One deadline is a target (goal) and the other is a hard limit (floor\n  or ceiling).** If so, which is which, and what are the consequences of\n  meeting only the weaker constraint?\n- **(d) The two requirements belong to different operating modes or\n  configurations** (for example, normal load vs. degraded mode). If so, how\n  is each mode defined and signalled at the boundary?\n\n*This question must be answered before either candidate can advance beyond\n`needs clarification`.*\n\n**Q2 (Definition of \"valid report request\"):** What makes a report request\nvalid? Specifically: is validity determined by the service itself, by an\nupstream system, or by both? What observable property at the service boundary\ndistinguishes a valid request from an invalid one?\n\n**Q3 (Definition of \"return the report\"):** Does \"return the report\" mean the\nfull report payload is delivered in the same synchronous response, or is an\nacknowledgement with a reference (e.g., a job ID for asynchronous retrieval)\nalso acceptable? The deadline meaning differs materially between the two\ninterpretations.\n\n**Q4 (Deadline measurement):** From which point is the deadline measured —\nthe moment the service receives the request, the moment the service begins\nprocessing, or some other point? To which point is it measured — the moment\nthe first byte of the response is sent, or the moment the complete response is\ndelivered?\n\n**Q5 (Concurrent and high-load behavior):** Does the deadline apply to every\nindividual report request independently, or only when the service is operating\nbelow a defined load threshold? If the deadline applies under all conditions,\nwhat happens when it cannot be met (is there a failure-path obligation)?\n\n---\n\n## Candidate Requirements\n\n### Candidate 1 (derived from R1)\n\n- **Status:** needs clarification\n- **Template:** Event-driven\n- **Requirement:** *(blocked — conflict with Candidate 2; see Consistency\n  Findings)*\n\n  Provisional (do not implement until conflict is resolved):\n  > When a valid report request arrives, the report service shall return the\n  > report within 1 second.\n\n- **Source intent and rationale:** R1 asserts a 1-second response deadline for\n  any valid report request to the report service. The rationale for choosing\n  1 second over 5 seconds is not stated in the source material.\n\n- **Implementability contract**\n  - System boundary: The public interface of the report service where valid\n    report requests are received and reports are returned.\n  - Responsible system: The report service.\n  - Actor: Any caller that submits a valid report request.\n  - Affected interfaces: The request-receipt interface and the\n    report-return interface of the report service.\n  - Inputs and data definitions: \"Valid report request\" — definition open\n    (see Q2). \"Report\" — payload definition open (see Q3).\n  - Preconditions, state, feature scope, and trigger: Trigger is the arrival\n    of a valid report request. No precondition or feature scope stated.\n  - Observable normal, alternate, and failure response: Normal — report\n    returned within 1 second. Alternate and failure paths undefined (see Q5).\n  - Quantities, units, limits, timing, ordering, and tolerances: 1 second.\n    Measurement start and end points open (see Q4). Tolerance undefined.\n  - Domain terms, dependencies, and unresolved decisions: **Direct conflict\n    with R2 (5-second deadline for the same trigger and system). Resolution\n    required (see Q1).** \"Valid report request\" undefined (Q2). Delivery\n    mode undefined (Q3). Measurement points undefined (Q4). Failure-path\n    behavior undefined (Q5).\n\n- **Verification contract**\n  - Setup, state, input data, and stimulus: Submit a valid report request to\n    the report service.\n  - Observable evidence or oracle: The elapsed time between the defined start\n    point and the defined end point of the service's response.\n  - Expected result: The complete report is returned within 1 second.\n  - Pass/fail criteria, including scope, thresholds, units, and timing: Pass\n    if elapsed time ≤ 1 second; fail otherwise. Measurement points and\n    tolerance are open (Q4).\n  - Boundary, negative, and failure cases: Behavior at exactly 1 second\n    undefined. Behavior when the deadline cannot be met is undefined (Q5).\n  - Required data, instrumentation, assessment, or external evidence: A\n    clock or timer observable at the service boundary; definition of \"valid\n    report request\" for test setup; definition of \"return the report\" to\n    identify the end event.\n  - Observable at the named boundary alone: yes — provided measurement\n    points are defined.\n  - If no, internal evidence needed and rationale: N/A (conditional on Q4\n    resolution).\n\n---\n\n### Candidate 2 (derived from R2)\n\n- **Status:** needs clarification\n- **Template:** Event-driven\n- **Requirement:** *(blocked — conflict with Candidate 1; see Consistency\n  Findings)*\n\n  Provisional (do not implement until conflict is resolved):\n  > When a valid report request arrives, the report service shall return the\n  > report within 5 seconds.\n\n- **Source intent and rationale:** R2 asserts a 5-second response deadline for\n  any valid report request to the report service. The rationale for choosing\n  5 seconds over 1 second is not stated in the source material.\n\n- **Implementability contract**\n  - System boundary: The public interface of the report service where valid\n    report requests are received and reports are returned.\n  - Responsible system: The report service.\n  - Actor: Any caller that submits a valid report request.\n  - Affected interfaces: The request-receipt interface and the\n    report-return interface of the report service.\n  - Inputs and data definitions: \"Valid report request\" — definition open\n    (see Q2). \"Report\" — payload definition open (see Q3).\n  - Preconditions, state, feature scope, and trigger: Trigger is the arrival\n    of a valid report request. No precondition or feature scope stated.\n  - Observable normal, alternate, and failure response: Normal — report\n    returned within 5 seconds. Alternate and failure paths undefined (see Q5).\n  - Quantities, units, limits, timing, ordering, and tolerances: 5 seconds.\n    Measurement start and end points open (see Q4). Tolerance undefined.\n  - Domain terms, dependencies, and unresolved decisions: **Direct conflict\n    with R1 (1-second deadline for the same trigger and system). Resolution\n    required (see Q1).** \"Valid report request\" undefined (Q2). Delivery\n    mode undefined (Q3). Measurement points undefined (Q4). Failure-path\n    behavior undefined (Q5).\n\n- **Verification contract**\n  - Setup, state, input data, and stimulus: Submit a valid report request to\n    the report service.\n  - Observable evidence or oracle: The elapsed time between the defined start\n    point and the defined end point of the service's response.\n  - Expected result: The complete report is returned within 5 seconds.\n  - Pass/fail criteria, including scope, thresholds, units, and timing: Pass\n    if elapsed time ≤ 5 seconds; fail otherwise. Measurement points and\n    tolerance are open (Q4).\n  - Boundary, negative, and failure cases: Behavior at exactly 5 seconds\n    undefined. Behavior when the deadline cannot be met is undefined (Q5).\n  - Required data, instrumentation, assessment, or external evidence: A\n    clock or timer observable at the service boundary; definition of \"valid\n    report request\" for test setup; definition of \"return the report\" to\n    identify the end event.\n  - Observable at the named boundary alone: yes — provided measurement\n    points are defined.\n  - If no, internal evidence needed and rationale: N/A (conditional on Q4\n    resolution).\n\n---\n\n## Suggested Supporting Requirements\n\n**SR-A — Failure-path companion (for whichever deadline is confirmed)**\n*Source behavior:* The confirmed deadline requirement (Candidate 1 or 2).\n*Missing behavior:* No requirement defines what the report service shall do\nwhen it cannot return the report within the deadline.\n*Reason:* Without a failure-path obligation, two implementers could choose\ndifferent visible behaviors (silent timeout, error response, partial result,\nretry) and both would be compliant. This creates unverifiable behavior.\n*Suggested form (provisional):* `If the report service cannot return the\nreport within <confirmed deadline>, then the report service shall return an\nerror response to the caller indicating that the request could not be\ncompleted within the required time.`\n\n**SR-B — Boundary companion (for whichever deadline is confirmed)**\n*Source behavior:* The confirmed deadline requirement.\n*Missing behavior:* No requirement defines what \"valid report request\" means\nat the service boundary.\n*Reason:* Without this definition, a verifier cannot construct a conforming\ntest stimulus, and an implementer cannot determine which requests the deadline\napplies to. If the deadline applies only to valid requests, invalid-request\nbehavior is also undefined.\n*Suggested form (provisional):* A separate requirement or definition\nspecifying the observable properties that distinguish a valid report request\nfrom an invalid one, and the service's required response to an invalid request.\n\n**SR-C — Operational companion (conditional on Q5)**\n*Source behavior:* The confirmed deadline requirement.\n*Missing behavior:* No requirement constrains the workload or concurrency\nconditions under which the deadline must hold.\n*Reason:* Without a stated load condition, a verifier cannot determine whether\na deadline breach under high concurrency is a failure or within the allowed\noperating envelope. This is an optional consideration — ask whether a maximum\nconcurrent-request or throughput bound is in scope.\n\n---\n\n## Consistency Findings\n\n### Finding 1 — **Conflict (blocking)**\n\n| Property | Candidate 1 (R1) | Candidate 2 (R2) |\n|----------|-----------------|-----------------|\n| System | Report service | Report service |\n| Trigger | Valid report request arrives | Valid report request arrives |\n| Response | Return the report | Return the report |\n| Deadline | **1 second** | **5 seconds** |\n\n**Consequence:** Both requirements apply to exactly the same trigger, system,\nand response. No system can simultaneously satisfy a 1-second deadline and a\n5-second deadline as independent normative obligations for the same event —\nsatisfying the 1-second constraint satisfies both, but the 5-second constraint\nadds no independent normative content; if the 1-second constraint is binding,\nthe 5-second figure is vacuous. If the 5-second constraint is binding and the\n1-second figure is a goal rather than an obligation, then R1 is not a\nnormative requirement as written. In either case, the two cannot coexist as\nindependently normative requirements without one being vacuous or one being\nviolated by a conforming implementation.\n\n**Resolution options:**\n\n1. **Supersede:** Confirm that one deadline is correct and withdraw the other.\n   Identify which requirement is superseded and update traceability.\n2. **Partition by workload class:** Introduce a property of the request (e.g.,\n   report type, data volume, cache state) that routes requests to the\n   1-second or 5-second obligation. Both candidates are then re-scoped and\n   can coexist without conflict.\n3. **Reframe as target and limit:** Designate one deadline as a performance\n   goal (non-normative) and the other as the normative hard limit. The\n   goal-level statement should be removed from the requirement set or clearly\n   labelled non-normative.\n4. **Operating-mode split:** If one deadline applies in a degraded or\n   maintenance mode, introduce an explicit state precondition so the two\n   obligations are mutually exclusive.\n\n*Neither candidate can advance to `candidate` or `ready for review` until\nthis conflict is resolved.*\n\n---\n\n## Assumptions\n\n**A1:** Both R1 and R2 are intended to be normative obligations, not one being\na goal and the other a limit, unless the user confirms otherwise (see Q1-c).\n\n**A2:** \"Return the report\" means a complete, usable report payload is\ndelivered in the response — not merely an acknowledgement or a reference for\nasynchronous retrieval — unless the user confirms otherwise (see Q3).\n\n**A3:** The deadline is measured from request receipt to complete response\ndelivery at the service boundary, unless the user defines different measurement\npoints (see Q4).\n\n**A4:** The deadline applies to each individual report request independently\nof concurrent load, unless the user introduces a load condition (see Q5).\n\n---\n\n## Rationale and Context\n\nThe core problem is a **direct deadline conflict** on a single observable\nbehavior. Such conflicts are common when requirements originate from different\nsources (e.g., a UX team setting a user-experience target and an operations\nteam setting an infrastructure SLA), when a requirement is revised without\nretiring the prior version, or when different stakeholders have different\nmental models of what \"the report service\" processes.\n\nThe resolution must come from the requester: the skill cannot invent a\nprecedence rule. The most important action is answering Q1. All other\nquestions (Q2–Q5) are secondary but will block the readiness gate for any\nresulting candidates if left unanswered.\n\n---\n\n## Review Status\n\n| Candidate | Status | Blocking item |\n|-----------|--------|---------------|\n| Candidate 1 (R1, 1 s) | **needs clarification** | Conflict with R2 (Finding 1); Q1 unanswered; Q2, Q3, Q4, Q5 open |\n| Candidate 2 (R2, 5 s) | **needs clarification** | Conflict with R1 (Finding 1); Q1 unanswered; Q2, Q3, Q4, Q5 open |\n\n**No candidate is ready for review.** The blocking conflict (Finding 1) must\nbe resolved before either candidate can be refined, and Q2–Q5 must be\nanswered before either can pass the readiness gate.\n"},"output":"Created file successfully: artifacts/result.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: artifacts/result.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\": \"/private/var/folders/qm/d729jn6n47z5nxpkpf361l9w0000gn/T/agent-eval/v2-regression/cases/case-018-inconsistent-deadlines/artifacts/result.md\", \"content\": \"# Requirements Elicitation Package\\n\\n## Understanding\\n\\n**Requested outcome:** The report service must return a report within a defined\\ntime limit whenever a valid report request arrives.\\n\\n**Proposed system boundary:** The report service — the component that receives\\na valid report request at its public interface and returns the corresponding\\nreport to the caller.\\n\\n**Input artifact identification:**\\n\\n| Label | Text | Kind |\\n|-------|------|------|\\n| R1 | \\\"When a valid report request arrives, the report service shall return the report within 1 second.\\\" | Behavioral obligation (event-driven, with timing constraint) |\\n| R2 | \\\"When a valid report request arrives, the report service shall return the report within 5 seconds.\\\" | Behavioral obligation (event-driven, with timing constraint) |\\n\\nBoth R1 and R2 assert the same trigger (`a valid report request arrives`),\\nthe same system (`the report service`), and the same response (`return the\\nreport`), but they specify contradictory deadlines: **1 second** versus\\n**5 seconds**. The user has confirmed that both apply to the same service\\nboundary and workload. This is a direct conflict. Neither requirement can be\\naccepted as-is; both are held at `needs clarification` until the conflict is\\nresolved.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Clarifying Questions\\n\\n**Q1 (Conflict resolution — blocking):** R1 and R2 require the same response\\nfor the same event on the same system but specify different deadlines (1 s and\\n5 s). They cannot both be correct as written. Which of the following best\\ndescribes the intent?\\n\\n- **(a) One deadline replaces the other.** If so, which deadline is correct —\\n  1 second or 5 seconds — and which requirement should be superseded?\\n- **(b) The two deadlines apply to different workload or report classes** (for\\n  example, simple vs. complex reports, small vs. large datasets, cached vs.\\n  uncached results). If so, how should each class be identified at the service\\n  boundary so that a verifier can distinguish them?\\n- **(c) One deadline is a target (goal) and the other is a hard limit (floor\\n  or ceiling).** If so, which is which, and what are the consequences of\\n  meeting only the weaker constraint?\\n- **(d) The two requirements belong to different operating modes or\\n  configurations** (for example, normal load vs. degraded mode). If so, how\\n  is each mode defined and signalled at the boundary?\\n\\n*This question must be answered before either candidate can advance beyond\\n`needs clarification`.*\\n\\n**Q2 (Definition of \\\"valid report request\\\"):** What makes a report request\\nvalid? Specifically: is validity determined by the service itself, by an\\nupstream system, or by both? What observable property at the service boundary\\ndistinguishes a valid request from an invalid one?\\n\\n**Q3 (Definition of \\\"return the report\\\"):** Does \\\"return the report\\\" mean the\\nfull report payload is delivered in the same synchronous response, or is an\\nacknowledgement with a reference (e.g., a job ID for asynchronous retrieval)\\nalso acceptable? The deadline meaning differs materially between the two\\ninterpretations.\\n\\n**Q4 (Deadline measurement):** From which point is the deadline measured —\\nthe moment the service receives the request, the moment the service begins\\nprocessing, or some other point? To which point is it measured — the moment\\nthe first byte of the response is sent, or the moment the complete response is\\ndelivered?\\n\\n**Q5 (Concurrent and high-load behavior):** Does the deadline apply to every\\nindividual report request independently, or only when the service is operating\\nbelow a defined load threshold? If the deadline applies under all conditions,\\nwhat happens when it cannot be met (is there a failure-path obligation)?\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Candidate Requirements\\n\\n### Candidate 1 (derived from R1)\\n\\n- **Status:** needs clarification\\n- **Template:** Event-driven\\n- **Requirement:** *(blocked — conflict with Candidate 2; see Consistency\\n  Findings)*\\n\\n  Provisional (do not implement until conflict is resolved):\\n  > When a valid report request arrives, the report service shall return the\\n  > report within 1 second.\\n\\n- **Source intent and rationale:** R1 asserts a 1-second response deadline for\\n  any valid report request to the report service. The rationale for choosing\\n  1 second over 5 seconds is not stated in the source material.\\n\\n- **Implementability contract**\\n  - System boundary: The public interface of the report service where valid\\n    report requests are received and reports are returned.\\n  - Responsible system: The report service.\\n  - Actor: Any caller that submits a valid report request.\\n  - Affected interfaces: The request-receipt interface and the\\n    report-return interface of the report service.\\n  - Inputs and data definitions: \\\"Valid report request\\\" — definition open\\n    (see Q2). \\\"Report\\\" — payload definition open (see Q3).\\n  - Preconditions, state, feature scope, and trigger: Trigger is the arrival\\n    of a valid report request. No precondition or feature scope stated.\\n  - Observable normal, alternate, and failure response: Normal — report\\n    returned within 1 second. Alternate and failure paths undefined (see Q5).\\n  - Quantities, units, limits, timing, ordering, and tolerances: 1 second.\\n    Measurement start and end points open (see Q4). Tolerance undefined.\\n  - Domain terms, dependencies, and unresolved decisions: **Direct conflict\\n    with R2 (5-second deadline for the same trigger and system). Resolution\\n    required (see Q1).** \\\"Valid report request\\\" undefined (Q2). Delivery\\n    mode undefined (Q3). Measurement points undefined (Q4). Failure-path\\n    behavior undefined (Q5).\\n\\n- **Verification contract**\\n  - Setup, state, input data, and stimulus: Submit a valid report request to\\n    the report service.\\n  - Observable evidence or oracle: The elapsed time between the defined start\\n    point and the defined end point of the service's response.\\n  - Expected result: The complete report is returned within 1 second.\\n  - Pass/fail criteria, including scope, thresholds, units, and timing: Pass\\n    if elapsed time ≤ 1 second; fail otherwise. Measurement points and\\n    tolerance are open (Q4).\\n  - Boundary, negative, and failure cases: Behavior at exactly 1 second\\n    undefined. Behavior when the deadline cannot be met is undefined (Q5).\\n  - Required data, instrumentation, assessment, or external evidence: A\\n    clock or timer observable at the service boundary; definition of \\\"valid\\n    report request\\\" for test setup; definition of \\\"return the report\\\" to\\n    identify the end event.\\n  - Observable at the named boundary alone: yes — provided measurement\\n    points are defined.\\n  - If no, internal evidence needed and rationale: N/A (conditional on Q4\\n    resolution).\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Candidate 2 (derived from R2)\\n\\n- **Status:** needs clarification\\n- **Template:** Event-driven\\n- **Requirement:** *(blocked — conflict with Candidate 1; see Consistency\\n  Findings)*\\n\\n  Provisional (do not implement until conflict is resolved):\\n  > When a valid report request arrives, the report service shall return the\\n  > report within 5 seconds.\\n\\n- **Source intent and rationale:** R2 asserts a 5-second response deadline for\\n  any valid report request to the report service. The rationale for choosing\\n  5 seconds over 1 second is not stated in the source material.\\n\\n- **Implementability contract**\\n  - System boundary: The public interface of the report service where valid\\n    report requests are received and reports are returned.\\n  - Responsible system: The report service.\\n  - Actor: Any caller that submits a valid report request.\\n  - Affected interfaces: The request-receipt interface and the\\n    report-return interface of the report service.\\n  - Inputs and data definitions: \\\"Valid report request\\\" — definition open\\n    (see Q2). \\\"Report\\\" — payload definition open (see Q3).\\n  - Preconditions, state, feature scope, and trigger: Trigger is the arrival\\n    of a valid report request. No precondition or feature scope stated.\\n  - Observable normal, alternate, and failure response: Normal — report\\n    returned within 5 seconds. Alternate and failure paths undefined (see Q5).\\n  - Quantities, units, limits, timing, ordering, and tolerances: 5 seconds.\\n    Measurement start and end points open (see Q4). Tolerance undefined.\\n  - Domain terms, dependencies, and unresolved decisions: **Direct conflict\\n    with R1 (1-second deadline for the same trigger and system). Resolution\\n    required (see Q1).** \\\"Valid report request\\\" undefined (Q2). Delivery\\n    mode undefined (Q3). Measurement points undefined (Q4). Failure-path\\n    behavior undefined (Q5).\\n\\n- **Verification contract**\\n  - Setup, state, input data, and stimulus: Submit a valid report request to\\n    the report service.\\n  - Observable evidence or oracle: The elapsed time between the defined start\\n    point and the defined end point of the service's response.\\n  - Expected result: The complete report is returned within 5 seconds.\\n  - Pass/fail criteria, including scope, thresholds, units, and timing: Pass\\n    if elapsed time ≤ 5 seconds; fail otherwise. Measurement points and\\n    tolerance are open (Q4).\\n  - Boundary, negative, and failure cases: Behavior at exactly 5 seconds\\n    undefined. Behavior when the deadline cannot be met is undefined (Q5).\\n  - Required data, instrumentation, assessment, or external evidence: A\\n    clock or timer observable at the service boundary; definition of \\\"valid\\n    report request\\\" for test setup; definition of \\\"return the report\\\" to\\n    identify the end event.\\n  - Observable at the named boundary alone: yes — provided measurement\\n    points are defined.\\n  - If no, internal evidence needed and rationale: N/A (conditional on Q4\\n    resolution).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Suggested Supporting Requirements\\n\\n**SR-A — Failure-path companion (for whichever deadline is confirmed)**\\n*Source behavior:* The confirmed deadline requirement (Candidate 1 or 2).\\n*Missing behavior:* No requirement defines what the report service shall do\\nwhen it cannot return the report within the deadline.\\n*Reason:* Without a failure-path obligation, two implementers could choose\\ndifferent visible behaviors (silent timeout, error response, partial result,\\nretry) and both would be compliant. This creates unverifiable behavior.\\n*Suggested form (provisional):* `If the report service cannot return the\\nreport within <confirmed deadline>, then the report service shall return an\\nerror response to the caller indicating that the request could not be\\ncompleted within the required time.`\\n\\n**SR-B — Boundary companion (for whichever deadline is confirmed)**\\n*Source behavior:* The confirmed deadline requirement.\\n*Missing behavior:* No requirement defines what \\\"valid report request\\\" means\\nat the service boundary.\\n*Reason:* Without this definition, a verifier cannot construct a conforming\\ntest stimulus, and an implementer cannot determine which requests the deadline\\napplies to. If the deadline applies only to valid requests, invalid-request\\nbehavior is also undefined.\\n*Suggested form (provisional):* A separate requirement or definition\\nspecifying the observable properties that distinguish a valid report request\\nfrom an invalid one, and the service's required response to an invalid request.\\n\\n**SR-C — Operational companion (conditional on Q5)**\\n*Source behavior:* The confirmed deadline requirement.\\n*Missing behavior:* No requirement constrains the workload or concurrency\\nconditions under which the deadline must hold.\\n*Reason:* Without a stated load condition, a verifier cannot determine whether\\na deadline breach under high concurrency is a failure or within the allowed\\noperating envelope. This is an optional consideration — ask whether a maximum\\nconcurrent-request or throughput bound is in scope.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Consistency Findings\\n\\n### Finding 1 — **Conflict (blocking)**\\n\\n| Property | Candidate 1 (R1) | Candidate 2 (R2) |\\n|----------|-----------------|-----------------|\\n| System | Report service | Report service |\\n| Trigger | Valid report request arrives | Valid report request arrives |\\n| Response | Return the report | Return the report |\\n| Deadline | **1 second** | **5 seconds** |\\n\\n**Consequence:** Both requirements apply to exactly the same trigger, system,\\nand response. No system can simultaneously satisfy a 1-second deadline and a\\n5-second deadline as independent normative obligations for the same event —\\nsatisfying the 1-second constraint satisfies both, but the 5-second constraint\\nadds no independent normative content; if the 1-second constraint is binding,\\nthe 5-second figure is vacuous. If the 5-second constraint is binding and the\\n1-second figure is a goal rather than an obligation, then R1 is not a\\nnormative requirement as written. In either case, the two cannot coexist as\\nindependently normative requirements without one being vacuous or one being\\nviolated by a conforming implementation.\\n\\n**Resolution options:**\\n\\n1. **Supersede:** Confirm that one deadline is correct and withdraw the other.\\n   Identify which requirement is superseded and update traceability.\\n2. **Partition by workload class:** Introduce a property of the request (e.g.,\\n   report type, data volume, cache state) that routes requests to the\\n   1-second or 5-second obligation. Both candidates are then re-scoped and\\n   can coexist without conflict.\\n3. **Reframe as target and limit:** Designate one deadline as a performance\\n   goal (non-normative) and the other as the normative hard limit. The\\n   goal-level statement should be removed from the requirement set or clearly\\n   labelled non-normative.\\n4. **Operating-mode split:** If one deadline applies in a degraded or\\n   maintenance mode, introduce an explicit state precondition so the two\\n   obligations are mutually exclusive.\\n\\n*Neither candidate can advance to `candidate` or `ready for review` until\\nthis conflict is resolved.*\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Assumptions\\n\\n**A1:** Both R1 and R2 are intended to be normative obligations, not one being\\na goal and the other a limit, unless the user confirms otherwise (see Q1-c).\\n\\n**A2:** \\\"Return the report\\\" means a complete, usable report payload is\\ndelivered in the response — not merely an acknowledgement or a reference for\\nasynchronous retrieval — unless the user confirms otherwise (see Q3).\\n\\n**A3:** The deadline is measured from request receipt to complete response\\ndelivery at the service boundary, unless the user defines different measurement\\npoints (see Q4).\\n\\n**A4:** The deadline applies to each individual report request independently\\nof concurrent load, unless the user introduces a load condition (see Q5).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Rationale and Context\\n\\nThe core problem is a **direct deadline conflict** on a single observable\\nbehavior. Such conflicts are common when requirements originate from different\\nsources (e.g., a UX team setting a user-experience target and an operations\\nteam setting an infrastructure SLA), when a requirement is revised without\\nretiring the prior version, or when different stakeholders have different\\nmental models of what \\\"the report service\\\" processes.\\n\\nThe resolution must come from the requester: the skill cannot invent a\\nprecedence rule. The most important action is answering Q1. All other\\nquestions (Q2–Q5) are secondary but will block the readiness gate for any\\nresulting candidates if left unanswered.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Review Status\\n\\n| Candidate | Status | Blocking item |\\n|-----------|--------|---------------|\\n| Candidate 1 (R1, 1 s) | **needs clarification** | Conflict with R2 (Finding 1); Q1 unanswered; Q2, Q3, Q4, Q5 open |\\n| Candidate 2 (R2, 5 s) | **needs clarification** | Conflict with R1 (Finding 1); Q1 unanswered; Q2, Q3, Q4, Q5 open |\\n\\n**No candidate is ready for review.** The blocking conflict (Finding 1) must\\nbe resolved before either candidate can be refined, and Q2–Q5 must be\\nanswered before either can pass the readiness gate.\\n\"}"},"time":{"start":1791982371695,"end":1791982442441}}}}
