{"type":"text","timestamp":1791982498495,"sessionID":"ses_ec58729d2ffeZXCFNW02JwR47g","part":{"id":"prt_13a7a1c43001mAzIB4EbuE2b8T","sessionID":"ses_ec58729d2ffeZXCFNW02JwR47g","messageID":"msg_13a7a186500145GI1beNrAMvGL","type":"text","text":"The package is also written to `artifacts/result.md`. Here is the complete portable Requirements Elicitation Package:\n\n---\n\n# Requirements Elicitation Package\n\n## Understanding\n\n**Requested outcome:** The report service must return a report within a defined time limit whenever a valid report request arrives.\n\n**Proposed system boundary:** The report service — the component that receives a valid report request at its public interface and returns the corresponding report to the caller.\n\n**Input artifact identification:**\n\n| Label | Text | Kind |\n|-------|------|------|\n| R1 | \"When a valid report request arrives, the report service shall return the report within 1 second.\" | Behavioral obligation (event-driven, with timing constraint) |\n| R2 | \"When a valid report request arrives, the report service shall return the report within 5 seconds.\" | Behavioral obligation (event-driven, with timing constraint) |\n\nBoth R1 and R2 assert the same trigger (`a valid report request arrives`), the same system (`the report service`), and the same response (`return the report`), but they specify contradictory deadlines: **1 second** versus **5 seconds**. The user has confirmed that both apply to the same service boundary and workload. This is a direct conflict. Neither requirement can be accepted as-is; both are held at `needs clarification` until the conflict is resolved.\n\n---\n\n## Clarifying Questions\n\n**Q1 (Conflict resolution — blocking):** R1 and R2 require the same response for the same event on the same system but specify different deadlines (1 s and 5 s). They cannot both be correct as written. Which of the following best describes the intent?\n\n- **(a) One deadline replaces the other.** If so, which deadline is correct — 1 second or 5 seconds — and which requirement should be superseded?\n- **(b) The two deadlines apply to different workload or report classes** (for example, simple vs. complex reports, small vs. large datasets, cached vs. uncached results). If so, how should each class be identified at the service boundary so that a verifier can distinguish them?\n- **(c) One deadline is a target (goal) and the other is a hard limit.** If so, which is which, and what are the consequences of meeting only the weaker constraint?\n- **(d) The two requirements belong to different operating modes or configurations** (for example, normal load vs. degraded mode). If so, how is each mode defined and signalled at the boundary?\n\n*This question must be answered before either candidate can advance beyond `needs clarification`.*\n\n**Q2 (Definition of \"valid report request\"):** What makes a report request valid? Is validity determined by the service itself, by an upstream system, or by both? What observable property at the service boundary distinguishes a valid request from an invalid one?\n\n**Q3 (Definition of \"return the report\"):** Does \"return the report\" mean the full report payload is delivered in the same synchronous response, or is an acknowledgement with a reference (e.g., a job ID for asynchronous retrieval) also acceptable? The deadline meaning differs materially between the two interpretations.\n\n**Q4 (Deadline measurement):** From which point is the deadline measured — the moment the service receives the request, or when processing begins? To which point — the moment the first byte of the response is sent, or the moment the complete response is delivered?\n\n**Q5 (Concurrent and high-load behavior):** Does the deadline apply to every individual request independently, or only when the service is operating below a defined load threshold? What happens when the deadline cannot be met — is there a failure-path obligation?\n\n---\n\n## Candidate Requirements\n\n### Candidate 1 (derived from R1)\n\n- **Status:** needs clarification\n- **Template:** Event-driven\n- **Requirement:** *(blocked — direct conflict with Candidate 2; see Consistency Findings)*\n\n  Provisional (do not implement until conflict is resolved):\n  > When a valid report request arrives, the report service shall return the report within 1 second.\n\n- **Source intent and rationale:** R1 asserts a 1-second response deadline for any valid report request. The rationale for choosing 1 second over 5 seconds is not stated in the source material.\n\n- **Implementability contract**\n  - System boundary: The public interface of the report service where valid report requests are received and reports are returned.\n  - Responsible system: The report service.\n  - Actor: Any caller that submits a valid report request.\n  - Affected interfaces: The request-receipt interface and the report-return interface of the report service.\n  - Inputs and data definitions: \"Valid report request\" — definition open (Q2). \"Report\" — payload scope and delivery mode open (Q3).\n  - Preconditions, state, feature scope, and trigger: Trigger is the arrival of a valid report request. No precondition or feature scope stated.\n  - Observable normal, alternate, and failure response: Normal — report returned within 1 second. Alternate and failure paths undefined (Q5).\n  - Quantities, units, limits, timing, ordering, and tolerances: 1 second. Measurement start and end points open (Q4). Tolerance undefined.\n  - Domain terms, dependencies, and unresolved decisions: **Direct conflict with R2 (5-second deadline for the same trigger and system). Resolution required (Q1).** \"Valid report request\" undefined (Q2). Delivery mode undefined (Q3). Measurement points undefined (Q4). Failure-path behavior undefined (Q5).\n\n- **Verification contract**\n  - Setup, state, input data, and stimulus: Submit a valid report request to the report service.\n  - Observable evidence or oracle: Elapsed time between the defined start point and the defined end point of the service's response.\n  - Expected result: The complete report is returned within 1 second.\n  - Pass/fail criteria, including scope, thresholds, units, and timing: Pass if elapsed time ≤ 1 second; fail otherwise. Measurement points and tolerance are open (Q4).\n  - Boundary, negative, and failure cases: Behavior at exactly 1 second undefined. Behavior when the deadline cannot be met is undefined (Q5).\n  - Required data, instrumentation, assessment, or external evidence: A clock or timer observable at the service boundary; definition of \"valid report request\" for test setup; definition of \"return the report\" to identify the end event.\n  - Observable at the named boundary alone: yes — provided measurement points are defined.\n  - If no, internal evidence needed and rationale: N/A.\n\n---\n\n### Candidate 2 (derived from R2)\n\n- **Status:** needs clarification\n- **Template:** Event-driven\n- **Requirement:** *(blocked — direct conflict with Candidate 1; see Consistency Findings)*\n\n  Provisional (do not implement until conflict is resolved):\n  > When a valid report request arrives, the report service shall return the report within 5 seconds.\n\n- **Source intent and rationale:** R2 asserts a 5-second response deadline for any valid report request. The rationale for choosing 5 seconds over 1 second is not stated in the source material.\n\n- **Implementability contract**\n  - System boundary: The public interface of the report service where valid report requests are received and reports are returned.\n  - Responsible system: The report service.\n  - Actor: Any caller that submits a valid report request.\n  - Affected interfaces: The request-receipt interface and the report-return interface of the report service.\n  - Inputs and data definitions: \"Valid report request\" — definition open (Q2). \"Report\" — payload scope and delivery mode open (Q3).\n  - Preconditions, state, feature scope, and trigger: Trigger is the arrival of a valid report request. No precondition or feature scope stated.\n  - Observable normal, alternate, and failure response: Normal — report returned within 5 seconds. Alternate and failure paths undefined (Q5).\n  - Quantities, units, limits, timing, ordering, and tolerances: 5 seconds. Measurement start and end points open (Q4). Tolerance undefined.\n  - Domain terms, dependencies, and unresolved decisions: **Direct conflict with R1 (1-second deadline for the same trigger and system). Resolution required (Q1).** \"Valid report request\" undefined (Q2). Delivery mode undefined (Q3). Measurement points undefined (Q4). Failure-path behavior undefined (Q5).\n\n- **Verification contract**\n  - Setup, state, input data, and stimulus: Submit a valid report request to the report service.\n  - Observable evidence or oracle: Elapsed time between the defined start point and the defined end point of the service's response.\n  - Expected result: The complete report is returned within 5 seconds.\n  - Pass/fail criteria, including scope, thresholds, units, and timing: Pass if elapsed time ≤ 5 seconds; fail otherwise. Measurement points and tolerance are open (Q4).\n  - Boundary, negative, and failure cases: Behavior at exactly 5 seconds undefined. Behavior when the deadline cannot be met is undefined (Q5).\n  - Required data, instrumentation, assessment, or external evidence: A clock or timer observable at the service boundary; definition of \"valid report request\" for test setup; definition of \"return the report\" to identify the end event.\n  - Observable at the named boundary alone: yes — provided measurement points are defined.\n  - If no, internal evidence needed and rationale: N/A.\n\n---\n\n## Suggested Supporting Requirements\n\n**SR-A — Failure-path companion** (for whichever deadline is confirmed)\n*Source behavior:* The confirmed deadline requirement (Candidate 1 or 2).\n*Missing behavior:* No requirement defines what the report service shall do when it cannot return the report within the deadline.\n*Reason:* Without a failure-path obligation, two implementers could choose different visible behaviors (silent timeout, error response, partial result, retry) and both would be compliant. Suggested provisional form: `If the report service cannot return the report within <confirmed deadline>, then the report service shall return an error response to the caller indicating that the request could not be completed within the required time.`\n\n**SR-B — Boundary companion** (for whichever deadline is confirmed)\n*Source behavior:* The confirmed deadline requirement.\n*Missing behavior:* No requirement or definition specifies what constitutes a \"valid report request\" at the service boundary.\n*Reason:* Without this definition, a verifier cannot construct a conforming test stimulus, and an implementer cannot determine which requests the deadline applies to. The service's response to an invalid request is also undefined.\n\n**SR-C — Operational companion** (conditional on Q5)\n*Source behavior:* The confirmed deadline requirement.\n*Missing behavior:* No requirement constrains the workload or concurrency conditions under which the deadline must hold.\n*Reason:* Without a stated load condition, a verifier cannot determine whether a deadline breach under high concurrency is a failure or within the allowed operating envelope. Ask whether a maximum concurrent-request or throughput bound is in scope.\n\n---\n\n## Consistency Findings\n\n### Finding 1 — **Conflict (blocking)**\n\n| Property | Candidate 1 (R1) | Candidate 2 (R2) |\n|----------|-----------------|-----------------|\n| System | Report service | Report service |\n| Trigger | Valid report request arrives | Valid report request arrives |\n| Response | Return the report | Return the report |\n| Deadline | **1 second** | **5 seconds** |\n\n**Consequence:** Both requirements apply to exactly the same trigger, system, and response. No system can simultaneously satisfy both as independent normative obligations for the same event. If the 1-second constraint is binding, the 5-second figure is vacuous. If the 5-second figure is the true normative limit and 1 second is a non-normative goal, then R1 is not a valid `shall` requirement as written. In either case, the two cannot coexist as independently normative requirements without one being vacuous or violated by a conforming implementation.\n\n**Resolution options:**\n\n1. **Supersede:** Confirm which deadline is correct and withdraw the other.\n2. **Partition by workload class:** Introduce an observable property of the request that routes it to the 1-second or 5-second obligation, so both can coexist with non-overlapping scopes.\n3. **Reframe as target and limit:** Designate one deadline as a non-normative performance goal and the other as the normative hard limit; remove the goal from the `shall` requirement set.\n4. **Operating-mode split:** If one deadline applies in a degraded mode, introduce an explicit state precondition to make the two obligations mutually exclusive.\n\n*Neither candidate can advance to `candidate` or `ready for review` until this conflict is resolved.*\n\n---\n\n## Assumptions\n\n**A1:** Both R1 and R2 are intended as normative obligations (`shall`), not one being a goal and the other a limit, unless the user confirms otherwise (Q1-c).\n\n**A2:** \"Return the report\" means a complete, usable report payload delivered in the response — not an acknowledgement or asynchronous reference — unless the user confirms otherwise (Q3).\n\n**A3:** The deadline is measured from request receipt to complete response delivery at the service boundary, unless the user defines different measurement points (Q4).\n\n**A4:** The deadline applies to each individual report request independently of concurrent load, unless the user introduces a load condition (Q5).\n\n---\n\n## Rationale and Context\n\nThe central problem is a **direct deadline conflict** on a single observable behavior. Such conflicts commonly arise when requirements originate from different sources (e.g., a UX team setting a user-experience target and an operations team setting an infrastructure SLA), when a requirement is revised without retiring the prior version, or when different stakeholders hold different mental models of the service scope.\n\nThe resolution must come from the requester. The skill cannot invent a precedence rule. Answering Q1 is the single most important action. Q2–Q5 are secondary but will independently block the readiness gate for any resulting candidates if left unanswered.\n\n---\n\n## Review Status\n\n| Candidate | Status | Blocking items |\n|-----------|--------|----------------|\n| Candidate 1 (R1, 1 s) | **needs clarification** | Finding 1 (conflict); Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4, Q5 open |\n| Candidate 2 (R2, 5 s) | **needs clarification** | Finding 1 (conflict); Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4, Q5 open |\n\n**No candidate is ready for review.** The blocking conflict (Finding 1) must be resolved before either candidate can be refined, and Q2–Q5 must be answered before either can pass the readiness gate.","time":{"start":1791982443587,"end":1791982498495}}}
